Overall, IPv4 is the recommended IP scheme for the company - IPv4, alongside IPv6, is an established standard for IP addressing whose addresses comprise of four 8-bit numbers ranging between 1-255 which make up an address. I have recommended IPv4 for the company as they do not have complex network requirements, and would not see the true benefit of using IPv6 as they have a limited number of nodes requiring addressing. IPv6 has 2^128 number of total addresses available, roughly 7.9 * 10^28 times the amount of IPv4 addresses available (roughly 4.3 billion) - this is significantly more than the company needs.

Using the network topology created, the company would be able to purchase one actual external IP (between 0.0.0.0 and 255.255.255.255) whilst using internal IPs for all internal nodes, as they connect to the internet via a centralised router (which has a single IP assigned to it) - this cuts out the need for buying a high-class domain, and would allow the company to run on a single class-D public IP address.

Internally several ranges of IPs can be used for each sector of the business, keeping the devices segregated whilst allowing traffic to flow between devices. This would be achievable with IPv6, but would provide too high a number of potential addresses compared to the overall size of the company.

IPv4 is a universally-compatible IP addressing schema, which is tried-and-tested in the modern working world. It has decades of established infrastructure and support across a range of vendors and devices, meaning the company has a larger pool of potential network hardware it can purchase in order to create the designed network, preventing vendor-lock and inflated prices. As IPv6 is not universally rolled out, using an IPv6-only scheme would require dual-stacking IPv4 and IPv6 schemes to ensure outside traffic from external vendors can make its way into the network as intended, complicating network setup and ongoing maintenance as there is an increased risk of one of the stacks failing, and being trickier to diagnose and fix the issue - one change can affect potentially a range of actions unintentionally.

IPv4 is an easier overall address format to remember, as it is comprised of significantly less potential characters - this again makes debugging issues easier as internal (private) network addresses can be segregated into different classes, and is much more human-readable compared to IPv6 - for a small to medium enterprise this is more favourable over a wider range of very similar addresses.